<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Core must never reference System.Runtime.InteropServices, Avalonia, or
       any platform package. "Does this work on Fedora?" should be answerable
       by reading this file rather than by grepping the codebase.

       Core does now make network requests, which it did not before: fetching an
       icon theme moved here from the interface assembly, because it had no UI
       dependency and a second application was otherwise going to copy sixty
       lines of HttpClient to call the same unpacker. HttpClient is in the
       framework and behaves the same everywhere, so the rule above is intact —
       but the widening is deliberate rather than accidental, and this is the
       file where anybody would look to find that out. -->


  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Vaktari.Core</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- So the archive reader's stream plumbing can be tested directly. Two of
         its properties are invisible from outside — that reads fill their
         buffer, and that the position it reports is truthful — and both were
         wrong in ways no archive fixture caught. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vaktari.Core.Tests" />
    <!-- The UI's tests need the Proton locate seam for the same reason: a
         test of "the tool is not installed" must not change its answer when
         the machine running it installs the tool. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vaktari.Ui.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- **The first package Core has ever referenced, and only because .NET has
         no xz decoder at all.** Gzip and zip are in the framework; xz is not,
         and it is the format most themes on the KDE Store are published in, so
         the alternative is telling people to recompress an archive before
         Vaktari will read it.

         Chosen over the native liblzma bindings: this is pure managed, so it
         adds nothing to the installer payload and nothing per-platform. It
         brings no transitive dependencies of its own, it is MIT licensed, and
         its decoder was checked against a NativeAOT publish with TrimMode=full
         before being adopted — the exe really does decompress an xz, which is
         not something a build succeeding would have told us.

         Still no platform package, which is the rule this file exists to state. -->
    <PackageReference Include="SharpCompress" Version="0.50.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
